From: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
Subject: [PATCH] net: airoha: do not decrement TTL on flows bridged within one bridge

A frame that enters and leaves through two ports of the same bridge is
L2-forwarded and must keep its IP TTL.  The flowtable fast path only
decrements TTL for routed flows; the PPE was not making that distinction,
because a bridged flow reaches the inet flowtable through br_netfilter and
is encoded as PPE_PKT_TYPE_IPV4_HNAPT, indistinguishable by address type
from a routed one.

Patch 960 tried to fix this by clearing AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_TTL for
PPE_PKT_TYPE_BRIDGE, but that type is only reachable from a tc-flower rule
that matches on ethernet addresses explicitly; the nft flowtable never
emits one, so bridged flows kept the bit set and the hardware decremented
the TTL.  Measured on a Gemtek XR1710G: a frame entering lan2 with TTL 5
left phy0.0-ap0 with TTL 4.

Decide it from the devices instead: if the egress device is a bridge port
and the ingress device is a port of the same bridge, the flow stays in L2
and BIND_TTL is cleared.  Routed flows keep decrementing, which also keeps
the previous behaviour for flows that enter or leave the bridge.

Note this covers the hardware path only.  nf_flow_offload_ipv4_forward()
still calls ip_decrease_ttl() unconditionally, so flows that are not (yet)
bound to the PPE are still decremented by software.  Fixing that needs a
flowtable-family test in the generic fast path and is left for a separate
change.

Signed-off-by: OpenWrt <openwrt@example.org>
---
 drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c | 38 ++++++++++++++++++++++++++++----
 1 file changed, 32 insertions(+), 6 deletions(-)

--- a/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
+++ b/drivers/net/ethernet/airoha/airoha_ppe.c
@@ -586,7 +586,7 @@
 					struct airoha_foe_entry *hwe,
 					struct net_device *netdev, int type,
 					struct airoha_flow_data *data,
-					int l4proto, u8 dsfield)
+					int l4proto, u8 dsfield, bool bridged)
 {
 	u32 qdata = FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_SHAPER_ID, 0x7f), ports_pad, val;
 	int wlan_etype = -EINVAL, dsa_port = airoha_get_dsa_port(&netdev);
@@ -601,7 +601,12 @@
 	      FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_VLAN_LAYER, data->vlan.num) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_VPM, data->vlan.num) |
 	      FIELD_PREP(AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_PPPOE, data->pppoe.num);
-	if (type != PPE_PKT_TYPE_BRIDGE)
+	/* Bridged flows that stay inside one bridge are L2-forwarded: the
+	 * PPE must not decrement their TTL.  Everything else -- routed
+	 * flows, and flows crossing into or out of the bridge -- is a
+	 * router hop and keeps the decrement.
+	 */
+	if (type != PPE_PKT_TYPE_BRIDGE && !bridged)
 		val |= AIROHA_FOE_IB1_BIND_TTL;
 	hwe->ib1 = val;
 
@@ -1568,6 +1573,8 @@
 	struct flow_match_meta meta;
 	u8 dsfield = 0, l4proto = 0;
 	struct airoha_foe_entry hwe;
+	struct net_device *indev;
+	bool bridged;
 	int err, i, offload_type;
 	u16 addr_type = 0;
 
@@ -1672,8 +1679,28 @@
 	    !is_valid_ether_addr(data.eth.h_dest))
 		return -EINVAL;
 
+	/* A flow whose ingress and egress devices are both ports of the same
+	 * bridge never leaves L2, so the PPE must forward it without touching
+	 * the IP TTL.  Routing -- including entering or leaving the bridge --
+	 * is the only case that decrements.
+	 *
+	 * This cannot be told from the dissector keys: bridged packets reach
+	 * the inet flowtable through br_netfilter and are encoded as
+	 * IPV4_HNAPT, exactly like routed ones.
+	 */
+	bridged = false;
+	if (odev && netif_is_bridge_port(odev)) {
+		rcu_read_lock();
+		indev = dev_get_by_index_rcu(dev_net(odev),
+					     meta.key->ingress_ifindex);
+		bridged = indev && netif_is_bridge_port(indev) &&
+			  netdev_master_upper_dev_get_rcu(indev) ==
+			  netdev_master_upper_dev_get_rcu(odev);
+		rcu_read_unlock();
+	}
+
 	err = airoha_ppe_foe_entry_prepare(eth, &hwe, odev, offload_type,
-					   &data, l4proto, dsfield);
+					   &data, l4proto, dsfield, bridged);
 	if (err)
 		return err;
 
